From 2a84ca280935ba0e3234a1de9ea6683bfc478c92 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AuraLite <auralite@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:56:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VIDEO V5: the 16 MiB aperture + vbe_start_addr panning

The V4 close-out left two measured gaps: the VRAM window was the 800x600x4
display buffer rather than a real VGA aperture, and the dispi offsets were
clamped and readable but never consumed (the display always rendered from
offset 0).  Both are closed on the one code path they share (dispi_fixup's
offset fold).

The make_ramwindow at 0xD0000000 now allocates GPU_VRAM_BYTES (the full
16 MiB Bochs/QEMU std-VGA aperture); m->fb_w/fb_h stay the published
800x600x32 DISPLAY (boot_info.fb, the 0x00200000 marker, the firmware
probe -- all unmoved, measured byte-stable).  Everything that treated
fb_w*fb_h*4 as 'the VRAM size' -- the YRES clamp, the offset ladder, the
enable-edge clear, VIDEO_MEMORY_64K, the BANK mask -- now measures the
aperture exactly as QEMU's vram_size does: --vbe=1024x768x32 (the V4 ASan
repro) fits unclamped, oversized modes clamp one line short of the window
(16000x12000x32 -> YRES 262), MEMORY_64K reads 256, bank mask 255.

dispi_fixup now keeps QEMU's fold (vbe_start_addr = offset >> 2, DWORDS)
and vga_render_display consumes it: the visible surface renders from
m->fb + vbe_start_addr at the VBE line offset (VIRT_WIDTH > XRES advances
whole lines at the pitch), and reads past the aperture render black rather
than running off the window -- the offset clamp guards YRES rows while the
renderer draws fb_h, which test_vbe_start_addr_panning pins explicitly.

Vectors: reworked test_dispi_mem_bank + test_dispi_mode_clamped_to_vram
(16 MiB offset ladder: rung 1 8x1x8, rung 2 400x1x32, rung 3
1024x4096x32), new test_vbe_start_addr_panning (Y/X fold, pitch, bounded
tail).  NC: revert src/devices.c -> abort at 'MEMORY_64K == 256'.
Firmware lane byte-stable (GUEST_OK, marker, 800x600 PPM).

README feature lines and the ROADMAP ledger/queue land here as the phase
close-out for the A1-A3/N4/V5 stretch.
